		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Réponse à&#160;:  ERROR: UNION types text and integer cannot be matched]]></title>
		<link rel="alternate" href="http://forums.postgresql.fr/viewtopic.php?pid=24332#p24332"/>
		<content type="html"><![CDATA[<p>Le problème est justement que NULL n&#039;a pas de type, et qu&#039;un champ doit avoir un type. Donc à défaut d&#039;avoir pu trouver un type, NULL UNION NULL est résolu en text, probablement parce que c&#039;est le type qui posera le moins de soucis.</p>]]></content>
		<author>
			<name><![CDATA[rjuju]]></name>
			<uri>http://forums.postgresql.fr/profile.php?id=1368</uri>
		</author>
		<updated>2016-11-16T12:48:28Z</updated>
		<id>http://forums.postgresql.fr/viewtopic.php?pid=24332#p24332</id>
	</entry>
	<entry>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Réponse à&#160;:  ERROR: UNION types text and integer cannot be matched]]></title>
		<link rel="alternate" href="http://forums.postgresql.fr/viewtopic.php?pid=24331#p24331"/>
		<content type="html"><![CDATA[<p>The declared type of the i-th column of TR is determined by applying Subclause 9.3, &quot;Result of data type combinations&quot;, to the declared types of the i-th column of T1 and the i-th column of T2.<br />1. Null union null=null<br />2. Null union int=int.<br />Je ne vois aucune raison d&#039;assigner un type texte quand il n&#039;y en a pas.</p>]]></content>
		<author>
			<name><![CDATA[albourg]]></name>
			<uri>http://forums.postgresql.fr/profile.php?id=7029</uri>
		</author>
		<updated>2016-11-16T12:40:12Z</updated>
		<id>http://forums.postgresql.fr/viewtopic.php?pid=24331#p24331</id>
	</entry>
	<entry>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Réponse à&#160;:  ERROR: UNION types text and integer cannot be matched]]></title>
		<link rel="alternate" href="http://forums.postgresql.fr/viewtopic.php?pid=24330#p24330"/>
		<content type="html"><![CDATA[<p>Et bien ni oracle ni access ne respectent la norme SQL sur ce point là.</p><br /><p>Si vous voulez une référence : <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/message-id/9799.1302719551@sss.pgh.pa.us" rel="nofollow">https://www.postgresql.org/message-id/9 … .pgh.pa.us</a></p><br /><br /><div class="quotebox"><cite>Tom Lane a écrit&#160;:</cite><blockquote><div><p>The reason we haven&#039;t done it is that it looks like the SQL standard requires type resolution for set-ops to happen one pair of input relations at a time. See SQL:2008 7.13 &lt;query expression&gt;, in which everything that&#039;s said about UNION/INTERSECT/EXCEPT is phrased in terms of exactly two input subqueries; for instance INTERSECT&#039;s result type is defined in syntax rule 18b as:</p><br /><div class="quotebox"><cite>norme SQL a écrit&#160;:</cite><blockquote><div><p>The declared type of the i-th column of TR is determined by applying Subclause 9.3, &quot;Result of data type combinations&quot;, to the declared types of the i-th column of T1 and the i-th column of T2.</p></div></blockquote></div></div></blockquote></div>]]></content>
		<author>
			<name><![CDATA[rjuju]]></name>
